# .numinance

Written by `numi init`. Typed Numinance configuration for this repository (design section 8). Review it like code.

- `policy/` is the protected location: a document there carries authority only when it is read from the protected
  branch of the repository, so protect `.numinance/policy/**` with branch protection and CODEOWNERS. Agents cannot change
  their own gates.
- Toolchain: Python {{.PythonVersion}}, package manager {{.PackageManager}}, verifiers {{.VerifierText}}.
- CI: {{.CIText}}.
{{- if .TicketFile}}
- Tickets: they come from the ticket file the run is given (`numi v1a run --ticket FILE`). No ticketing binding is written.
{{- end}}
- Autonomy: PR only. The ladder level is `AUTONOMY_LEVEL_PR`: the agent opens pull requests and never merges.
  `PR_ONLY` is a separate thing, a state of the engine's breaker.
{{- if .OrgMode}}
- The organization documents (stage registry, grants, floors) were written to the organization policy repository
  you named with `--org-dir`. Validate with `numi validate --org THAT_DIR .` (engine build).
{{- else}}
- Floors: a floor set in a repository is refused, because floors come only from the org scope and control packs. With no
  organization policy repository the engine applies its shipped floor, which caps every change at PR (HIGH risk at
  SUGGEST). Only the org scope selects the stage registry and grants names, so here the stage registry is defined but not
  selected and the playbook, toolchain and adapter bindings are listed as outside the org-granted set. Run `numi init --org-dir DIR` to write
  the org documents.
{{- end}}

The kernel floors (separation of duties, plan before side effects, evidence by the runner only, autonomy and residency
ceilings and others) are part of the Numinance engine, always on, and no document in this tree or in an organization
repository can weaken them. Nothing here references them, because there is nothing to reference.
{{- if .Jenkins}}

Jenkins: the ciexecutor-jenkins provider exists in replay mode only (a live run is owed; not yet in the release bundle); see the comments in `policy/sdlc-profile.yaml`.
{{- if .Both}}
The repository profile binds GitHub Actions. The Jenkins binding is only a grant (an org AdapterSelector) or an inert
document, and a one-line edit of the profile could rebind to the Jenkins provider. That rebinding is the operator's
explicit edit, and `numi validate` does not check that a provider is installed, so it would pass and still run nothing.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
